From: Gezim <gezimos@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 20:45:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Kompakt: the auto brightness curve is the front light's and
 can be set and taught

Auto brightness accepts a frontlight curve.

A slider change in auto scales the frontlight curve.

A slider change while auto brightness is on adds a user point, and stock
inserts it into the curve and smooths everything above it so the curve
never falls. 0146's frontlight curve falls to off in bright light, so
after one slider change the light stayed at the user's level up to
sunlight (812 lux measured at a window, light held at 41%).

SimpleMappingStrategy now scales the whole curve by the ratio of the
user's brightness to the curve at the user's lux, so it passes through
the user's point and keeps its shape, including off. Where the curve is
below its lowest step at that lux the scale would be huge, and the stock
adjustment is used.

Turning auto brightness off forgets the slider point.

A slider change while auto brightness is on is kept as a user point in
the short term model, which stock only invalidates five minutes after
the screen goes off. Turning auto off and on again therefore came back
at the adjusted curve, not the configured one. The point is now reset
when the setting goes to manual, so auto on starts from the curve.

The auto brightness curve can be set by the user.

SimpleMappingStrategy ignored every brightness configuration, and
BrightnessConfiguration.Builder refused a curve whose brightness ever
falls, so the only way to change the frontlight curve (0146) was a new
image.

The strategy now takes a configuration as its curve. Its nits are the
backlight steps of config_autoBrightnessLcdBacklightValues, 1 off, and
null brings the overlay curve back. The Kompakt app saves the curve
through DisplayManager.setBrightnessConfiguration, which the display
manager keeps per user and applies again at boot.

The front light fade runs with animations off.

The automatic brightness moves the screen to a new level through
RampAnimator, which walks the value a little each frame at the configured
rate. Its step reads ValueAnimator.getDurationScale() and, at 0, sets the
target at once ("Animation off"). This phone keeps every animation scale at
0 (SettingsProvider defaults, 0161), so the ramp never ran here: a change
of room set the front light from one row's number to the next in one frame,
whatever config_brightness_ramp_rate_slow said.

The ramp is not a screen animation. It fades the front light, which on
e-ink repaints nothing, so the animator scale does not apply to it: the
step now runs at scale 1. With kompakt_brightness.xml's slow rate a change
of room walks every number between two rows over a few seconds, and the
slider's fast rate stays the framework's.

Brightness ramps with the colour fade off.

DisplayPowerController skips the brightness ramp while "the display content
is not visible", which it defines as the colour fade being enabled and
fully faded in. This phone disables the colour fade
(config_displayColorFadeDisabled, no fade animation on e-ink), so that test
is false forever and every brightness change, automatic or not, is applied
in one frame. The slow ramp rate (kompakt_brightness.xml) and 0164 could
never run; the kernel log showed L:87 then L:31 with nothing between.

With the colour fade off the content is always visible, so the test reads
that way, and the automatic brightness walks the ramp: a change of room
fades through every number between two rows.

The ambient light is averaged over two seconds.

AutomaticBrightnessController believes a change of light only once both
its averages of the light sensor, over the last 2 and the last 10
seconds, have crossed the hysteresis threshold. On this phone the sensor
reports within a quarter of a second, but the ten-second average put 3
to 4 seconds between a change of light and the first move of the front
light (2026-11-10, the sensor's events against the brightness events).
The horizons come only from a display device config, which a GSI has
none of, so the constants are the phone's: 2 seconds and half a second.
The light moves about half a second after the light changes, and the
sensor's jitter of a lux at the dim end is still averaged out.

The brightness slider teaches the auto brightness curve.

With auto brightness on, a slider release gives AutomaticBrightnessController
a user point: the ambient lux and the brightness chosen. Stock keeps it
only as a short-term adjustment, and the long-term learning Pixels do lives
in a Google app; BrightnessTracker, its source, never starts on a display
without a nits table, which this one has none of. The controller now sends
the point to the Kompakt app, which writes it into the saved brightness
configuration; saving resets the short-term point, and the curve then
gives the same level at that light every time.
